\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether atlas accuracy (68.9--85.9\%) reflects genuine statistical regularities across eight generalization axes (five below, three in Appendix).

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares ordinal depth estimation methods across all 9 atlas cells.
RF, XGBoost, and LightGBM on the same 19 features converge within 0.4\,pp per cell, confirming a feature-determined ceiling; on Pythia nucleus, CORAL \cite{coral2020} (78.1\%) and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} (78.2\%) match RF within 0.6\,pp ($p>0.05$).

\paragraph{Binary detectors and membership inference.}
Four methods designed for binary AI-text detection or membership inference, when calibrated for ordinal depth, all fall 43--48\,pp below RF (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}).
Min-K\% Prob \cite{shi2024_minkprob} (33.2\%) outperforms DetectGPT \cite{mitchell2023_detectgpt} (30.7\%) by 2.5\,pp, consistent with token-level log-probabilities encoding more depth information than perturbation-based curvature; both remain near chance.
CORAL ordinal calibration does not close the gap (Min-K\% CORAL d1--d3 BalAcc: 40.4\% vs.\ RF 71.6\%), confirming that the bottleneck is input signal, not calibration.
Binary detector scores (Binoculars, Fast-DetectGPT) repurposed as RF features reach 74.2\% on a single cell but average only 30--35\% across 9 cells (Appendix~\ref{app:detector_baselines}).
Adding Min-K\% probability as a 20th RF feature yields +0.48\,pp (78.4\%$\to$78.9\%); Min-K\% ranks \#3 by importance (0.095), below \texttt{p90\_ppl} (0.161) and \texttt{var\_surprisal} (0.126), while DetectGPT contributes negligibly (\#17, 0.026).
The gain is consistent across 8 of 9 cells but small, confirming that the 19 statistical features already capture the dominant ordinal signal.

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); zero-shot LLMs (DeepSeek V3.2, 36.2\%; Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, 30.4\%) also fail (43\,pp below the best RF cell; Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt}); depth signal resides in distributional statistics inaccessible from surface text.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Ordinal depth estimation: method comparison (mean across 9 atlas cells; DetectGPT: 8 of 9, Pythia nucleus excluded due to scoring instability). Chance = 25\% (4-class) / 33.3\% (d1--d3). Min-K\%: $K{=}20$, CORAL calibration. DetectGPT: gpt2-large scoring, t5-large perturbation ($n{=}5$). 5-fold CV; SD $\leq$ 0.9\,pp for feature-based methods.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
Method & 4-Cl Acc (\%) & d1--d3 BalAcc (\%) \\
\midrule
RF 19-feature (ours) & \textbf{78.4} & \textbf{71.6} \\
\midrule
Zero-shot LLM (best) & 36.2 & --- \\
Min-K\% Prob (CORAL) & 33.2 & 40.4 \\
DetectGPT (perturbation) & 30.7 & 24.3 \\
Random & 25.0 & 33.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Convergence confirms the signal resides in the features (three classifiers match within 0.8\,pp under cross-model transfer; Appendix~\ref{app:learned_estimator}).
Perplexity features alone recover 66.0\% ($-$0.5\,pp vs.\ full), while repetition/diversity features alone fall 13.6\,pp below; perplexity degradation is the dominant depth marker.
Raw perplexity alone performs at chance (25.1\%); the strongest single feature (\texttt{p90\_ppl}) reaches 58.3\% ($-$8.3\,pp vs.\ full); multi-dimensional characterization is required. Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 near-perfectly identified; errors concentrate at d2/d3 in-distribution and spread to d1--d3 under transfer (RF seed std $= 0.06$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:rf_seed}).
Cross-seed replication across all three model families confirms consistent feature rankings and depth ordering; absolute accuracy varies by $+$0.1 to $+$3.2\,pp (same direction), with 2/3 deltas statistically significant (Appendix~\ref{app:seed_replication}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.9\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 bal.\ acc: Pythia 49.9\%, OLMo 59.2\%, GPT-2~XL 57.2\% (mean 55.4\%). Ordinal quality: MAE\,=\,0.39, QWK\,=\,0.81 (QWK$_{\text{d1--d3}}$\,=\,0.53); 94.8\% of errors at $|\Delta| \leq 1$.}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% (2.7$\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline (d1--d3 balanced mean 55.4\%).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy} reports leave-one-strategy-out transfer.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean cross-strategy accuracy (59.1\%) falls 7\,pp below cross-model transfer (66.4\%); d1--d3 accuracy drops to 45.9\% (chance = 33\%).
The core finding is structural: training on nucleus + top-$k$ inverts the d1/d2 boundary under temperature (d1v2 AUC $<$ 0.5), because temperature scaling accelerates convergence to a low-entropy attractor (\S\ref{sec:method:theory}); depth-$d$ tail statistics under temperature therefore resemble nucleus depth-$(d{+}1)$.
Strategy confusion concentrates between temperature and top-$k$ (31.2\%), consistent with their opposing effects on the perplexity tail.
As a proof-of-concept, a two-stage pipeline (strategy identification at 57.5\% + depth classification) recovers 11.5\,pp, with a strategy-invariant floor of 67.6\% (Limitations); end-to-end PLMs lag RF by 20\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:e2e}).
Conditional decomposition reveals that the four-class accuracy gap between correct and wrong strategy routing is moderate ($+$4.6\,pp: 72.6\% vs.\ 68.0\%, $N{=}103{,}498$ / $76{,}502$) because d0 accuracy increases under misrouting (${\sim}$99.6\%); the d1--d3 balanced accuracy gap is 31.5\,pp (70.6\% vs.\ 39.1\%): cross-strategy difficulty is a feature-space misalignment at inter-depth boundaries, not uniform noise.

\paragraph{Prompted generation.}
Replicating the GPT-2~XL nucleus pipeline with topic-seeded generation yields 69.5\% (95\% CI $[68.9, 70.2]$, $-$13.8\,pp vs.\ BOS-seeded, $2.8\times$ chance), with monotonic AUC decay and identical top features (Appendix~\ref{app:prompted_gen}): prompt conditioning attenuates but does not eliminate the depth signal.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Preliminary Cross-Scale Observations}
\label{sec:gen:crossscale}

Extending to 7--8B under QLoRA, Llama-3.1-8B Instruct \cite{llama3} achieves 70.2\% ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}4$) and Qwen2.5-7B \cite{qwen2.5} 60.1\% ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{>}5$).
At 14B (Qwen2.5-14B, self-reference model), accuracy drops to 54.1\% ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}0$; d0v1 AUC\,=\,0.643), though d1--d3 discrimination persists (balanced accuracy 57.4\%, chance 33\%); the depth signal attenuates with scale but does not vanish (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}).
These results are confounded by simultaneous changes in quantization (4-bit QLoRA), sample size (500 vs.\ 5{,}000), decoding strategy (nucleus only), vocabulary size, and (for 14B) reference model; they should be treated as directional evidence rather than definitive conclusions.
A base-model control (Llama-3.1-8B, 59.9\%, non-monotonic AUC) vs.\ instruction-tuned (70.2\%, monotonic) suggests instruction tuning \emph{regularizes} recursive degradation; a 1.5B instruct control (47.4\%, $1.9\times$ chance) confirms at matched scale (Appendix~\ref{app:instruct_1p5b}; confounds in Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_confounds}).
Cross-model chains (84.75\%), cross-domain (arXiv 74.1\%), and LoRA rank sensitivity ($\Delta{=}4.65$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}) further confirm robustness.

